.TH VFORK 2 "November 15, 2026" "Galtzagorriak MINIX"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
vfork \- create a process without copying the caller
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft B
#include <unistd.h>

pid_t vfork(void)
.ft R
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Vfork
creates a new process like
.BR fork (2),
but does not copy the caller.  The child runs in the parent's memory and on
its stack, and the parent is suspended until the child calls
.BR execve (2)
or
.BR _exit (2).
Then the parent goes on and
.B vfork
returns the child's pid to it; in the child it returns 0.
.PP
On the Flipper there is no MMU: a
.B fork
keeps a shadow copy of the caller's data and stack until the child execs.
.B Vfork
needs neither the copy nor the memory for it, so a program can still be
started when memory is short.  If the caller's addresses are shared with
another process already (a background subshell), the call is an ordinary
.BR fork .
.PP
Because the child uses the parent's memory, between
.B vfork
and
.B exec
it may only call
.BR exec *,
.BR _exit ,
.BR dup2 ,
.BR close ,
.BR open ,
.BR chdir ,
.BR setuid ,
.BR setgid ,
.BR setsid
and
.BR signal ,
and change only its own local variables.  It must not return from the
function that called
.BR vfork ,
call
.BR exit (3)
(that flushes the parent's stdio buffers),
.BR malloc ,
or anything else that changes global data.  A
.B fork
or
.B vfork
in the child fails with EAGAIN.  Signals sent to the parent meanwhile are
delivered when it goes on.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
As for
.BR fork (2).
.SH ERRORS
As for
.BR fork (2).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR fork (2),
.BR execve (2),
.BR _exit (2).
.SH NOTES
Used by
.BR sh (1)
for simple commands (no assignments, no here documents), and by
.BR init ,
the launcher and
.BR pkg .
